# Feature Branching

Before starting **any new feature**, the agent must:

1. Check the current branch: `git branch --show-current`
2. If on `main` (or `master`), create and switch to a feature branch:
   ```
   git checkout -b feature/<NNN>-<short-kebab-description>
   ```
   where `<NNN>` is the next sequential three-digit number (e.g. `001`, `002`); check existing branches with `git branch -a` first.
3. All implementation commits go on that branch — **never commit feature work directly to `main`**.

## Branch naming

Branches include a zero-padded three-digit feature number followed by a short kebab description:

- `feature/<NNN>-<name>` — new functionality
- `fix/<NNN>-<name>` — bug fixes without new features
- `chore/<NNN>-<name>` — tooling, refactors, non-functional changes

The number is sequential starting at `001`. Before creating a branch, check existing branch names to determine the next number:

```
git branch -a
```

## Example

```
git checkout -b feature/001-mob-aggro-radius
git checkout -b fix/002-projectile-floor-bounds
git checkout -b chore/003-editmode-test-helpers
```

## Commit policy — stage only, never commit

The agent **must never run `git commit`**. Instead:

1. Stage all relevant files with `git add`.
2. Display the intended commit message (following the branch naming convention prefix, e.g. `fix: …`, `feat: …`, `chore: …`) as plain text so the user can review it.
3. Leave the working tree in a staged-but-uncommitted state; the user commits manually.

This applies everywhere — feature work, fixes, chores, cherry-picks, and any other git operations the agent performs.

## When the feature is done

- Do **not** merge the feature branch into `main` or `master` from the agent session (no `git merge`, no push of a merge to the default branch).
- Remind the user to open a PR and complete the merge through their usual review flow when they choose to.
